  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{6}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes.",
  "worked_solution": "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-5$ and $k=2,$ so the center is located at $(-5,2).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=25$ and ${b}^{2}=6.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=25,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=6,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=5,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 5=10,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{6}=2\\sqrt{6}.$",
